Falsifies `terminal_contract::verification::decode_refuses_exactly_the_words_with_a_reserved_bit`.

The defect: the reserved mask stops one bit short and leaves bit 55 out. A client can then set
bit 55 and the terminal serves the request, so a later version cannot give that bit a meaning
without breaking a client that already relies on it being ignored.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!`, on any word with bit 55 set and bits 54 to 32 clear.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
index a0b1ce1..dc39f6a 100644
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -208,7 +208,7 @@ pub const fn len(w0: u64) -> usize {
 }
 
 /// Bits 55:32 of a request's first word, which no operation gives a meaning.
-const RESERVED_REQUEST_BITS: u64 = ((1 << OPERATION_SHIFT) - 1) & !0xffff_ffff;
+const RESERVED_REQUEST_BITS: u64 = ((1 << (OPERATION_SHIFT - 1)) - 1) & !0xffff_ffff;
 
 /// **A request's first word as a server reads it**: its operation and its length or count, or
 /// `None` when any reserved bit (55:32) is set. A terminal answers `None` with [`BAD_REQUEST`]
